    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart.jpg" alt="Ibn Tumart" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_engraving_depicting_Ibn_Tumart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joan Serra y Pausas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate originated not as a dynastic succession or military coup but as a religious reform movement of extraordinary ideological intensity, born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1121 CE. Its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Muhammad ibn Tumart</a>, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> theologian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> who had studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the Islamic East and returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> preaching strict <em>tawhid</em>, the absolute unity of God, and denouncing the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid dynasty</a> as anthropomorphists who ascribed physical attributes to God and tolerated moral laxity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> 1121 he was proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, the divinely guided redeemer, by his followers, and he later established his base at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a> in the High Atlas. The movement's early years were marked by raids and skirmishes against the Almoravids rather than conventional warfare, and in 1130 an Almohad attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> ended in a heavy defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-buhayra">Battle of al-Buhayra</a>; Ibn Tumart died a few months later. His chief disciple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> concealed the founder's death for some two years to prevent the movement's disintegration before emerging as its leader. Abd al-Mu'min, rather than Ibn Tumart, was the true architect of the Almohad state. Over a reign of more than thirty years he steadily overwhelmed the Almoravids, captured their capital Marrakesh in 1147, assumed the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, the first Berber ruler to do so, and completed the conquest of the Maghreb from Morocco to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> by 1160. From 1146 Almohad forces also crossed into al-Andalus, and the absorption of its remaining independent Muslim principalities, the last being the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-mardanish">Ibn Mardanish</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, was completed under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a> in 1172.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valencia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valencia.jpg" alt="Valencia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malvarrosa_Beach,_Valencia,_Spain_(29812271043).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boris Dzhingarov, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate governed one of the most economically diverse empires of the medieval world, spanning the agricultural plains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, the pastoral Saharan fringe, and the urban civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> with its dense networks of cities, irrigated agriculture and artisan industries. The North African base produced wheat, barley and livestock across the Tell and the Moroccan plains, while trans-Saharan routes in gold and slaves provided revenues from the empire's southern periphery. The Andalusian economy was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s most productive component: the irrigated <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huertas</a></em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> generated agricultural surpluses; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, Seville and other cities supported sophisticated industries in textiles, metalwork and ceramics; and the maritime trade of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> connected Almohad ports to Genoese, Pisan and Egyptian commercial networks. Seville served as the caliphate's Iberian capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a>, begun in 1184 as the minaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-seville">Seville's Great Mosque</a> and completed under his son, stands as a monument to the caliphate's peak prosperity. The Almohad fiscal system imposed heavy demands on subject populations, provoking periodic resistance, and the dynasty's religious rigour, which confronted Jewish and Christian communities with a choice of conversion or exile, disrupted the intellectual and commercial networks that had made Andalusian cities the most cosmopolitan in the western Mediterranean. The cost of simultaneously maintaining forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> severely strained the empire's finances, a structural burden that contributed materially to its eventual fragmentation.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos.jpg" alt="Battle of Alarcos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ciudad_Real,_Spain_location.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad military was built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes that had coalesced around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>'s movement, supplemented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanata-berbers">Zanata</a> and Arab cavalry, Andalusian contingents and later Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> transformed this tribal base into a disciplined force through systematic organisation and the ideological motivation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>, creating an army capable of the sustained campaigning needed to conquer both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s military power reached its zenith under Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</a> (r. 1184–1199), who won the empire's most celebrated victory at the Battle of Alarcos on 18 July 1195, where his forces crushed the Castilian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>. The victory earned him the honorific al-Mansur, "the Victorious", and in the following campaigns his forces devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a>, raiding to the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in the deepest Muslim penetration of Christian territory for generations. Al-Mansur's death in 1199 and the succession of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-nasir">Muhammad al-Nasir</a> gave the Christian kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> time to consolidate and to form a coalition under papal crusading sanction, culminating in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> on 16 July 1212. The combined armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, joined by Portuguese and other crusading contingents, shattered the Almohad force; al-Nasir fled the field and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> the following year. This defeat broke the military mystique that had been a primary source of Almohad political cohesion, triggering the accelerating fragmentation that would extinguish the caliphate within sixty years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin.jpg" alt="Abd al-Mu'min" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdalmumin_ben_ali_nedroma_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Askelaadden, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad system of government was characterised by a fundamental tension between its founding ideology and the practical demands of administering a vast and diverse empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a> had conceived the movement as a theocratic enterprise in which religious correctness, as defined by himself as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, justified political authority, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s legitimacy derived from his role as defender and enforcer of true doctrine rather than from genealogy alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a> institutionalised this by establishing a hierarchy of Almohad faithful, drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> and other early tribal adherents, who formed a ruling class distinct from the general Muslim population, alongside trained cadres of <em>talaba</em> who propagated official doctrine. The caliphal court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> maintained a sophisticated administration shaped by the Andalusian bureaucratic tradition, and the dynasty adorned its cities with monuments such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque">Koutoubia Mosque</a> in Marrakesh, whose minaret remains one of the finest examples of Almohad architecture, the unfinished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasbah-of-the-udayas">Kasbah of the Udayas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur">Yaqub al-Mansur</a> patronised the philosophers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> (Ibn Rushd), making the court a centre of the highest medieval intellectual culture, although al-Mansur briefly disgraced Averroes in 1195 under pressure from conservative jurists. The structural weakness of the system was its ideological dependency: authority flowed from the claim that the caliph governed as the legitimate successor of the Mahdi Ibn Tumart. When Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-i-al-mamun">Idris I al-Ma'mun</a> publicly repudiated Ibn Tumart's Mahdi status around 1229, in a bid that also secured Castilian military support, he demolished the theological foundation on which Almohad rule rested and accelerated the disintegration that followed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque.jpg" alt="Koutoubia Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque">Koutoubia Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marokko0112_(retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rol1000, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate's historical impact was concentrated in two domains: military and intellectual. Militarily, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> mounted the last great Muslim offensive against Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> (1195) showed that a unified Muslim power could still threaten the advance of the Christian kingdoms. The subsequent defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Las Navas de Tolosa</a> (1212) marked the decisive turning point after which Muslim political power in Iberia entered terminal decline, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-cordoba">fall of Córdoba</a> (1236), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> (1248) and, more than two centuries later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> (1492). Intellectually, the Almohad court, particularly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i">Abu Yaqub Yusuf</a> and al-Mansur, was one of the most important centres of philosophical and scientific activity in the medieval world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a>, working under Almohad patronage, produced commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> that transformed European scholastic philosophy when transmitted through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation, influencing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and the wider course of medieval Western thought. The Almohad unification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, the only time it was united under a single Berber dynasty, created an administrative and commercial framework inherited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> successor states. Architecturally, the Almohad contribution to the western Islamic world was lasting: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koutoubia-mosque">Koutoubia Mosque</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-tower">Hassan Tower</a>, the gates and walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a> of Seville are among the finest surviving monuments of medieval Islamic architecture, and the Giralda's conversion into a cathedral bell tower after the Christian conquest of Seville made it a permanent fixture of the city's skyline.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa.jpg" alt="Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_las_Navas_de_Tolosa,_por_Francisco_van_Halen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Francisco de Paula Van Halen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Almohad Caliphate's decline began with a single catastrophic military defeat and accelerated through a sequence of institutional failures that progressively stripped the empire of its territorial, ideological and political coherence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212 broke not merely the Almohad army but the military prestige that underpinned caliphal authority: a dynasty whose legitimacy rested on divinely guaranteed victory could not absorb a crushing defeat without existential damage to its ideology. The death without heir of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ii">Yusuf II</a> (r. 1213–1224) produced rival caliphal claimants, provincial governors who allied with Christian kingdoms in exchange for local autonomy, and a rapid territorial unravelling. Around 1229 came a double blow: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafsid-dynasty">Hafsid</a> governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, Abu Zakariya, broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, and Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> repudiated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> status, removing the eastern third of the African domain and demolishing the theological foundation of the entire enterprise. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Zayyanids</a> established themselves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> around 1236, carving out the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-cordoba">fall of Córdoba</a> (1236) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> (1248) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> ended centuries of Muslim rule in the cities most closely associated with Andalusian civilisation. By the 1260s the Almohads held little beyond Marrakesh and its surroundings, and in 1269 the last caliph, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-al-wathiq">Idris al-Wathiq</a> (Abu Dabbus), was killed in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>, who seized the capital and extinguished the dynasty that had once ruled from the Atlantic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Almohad Caliphate's enduring significance lies in its role both as the last great unifier of the western Islamic world and, through the philosophy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Averroes</a> produced under its patronage, as an inadvertent catalyst of the European intellectual revival that would eventually transform the global balance of power.</em></p>

